{"name":"Root Beer","hex":"#81544a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"root beer","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A beverage, most often a carbonated soft drink, traditionally made from a combination of vanilla, cherry tree bark, licorice root, sarsaparilla root, sassafras root bark, nutmeg, anise, and molasses, among other flavorings.","A dark reddish-brown color, similar to the drink."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/root_beer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Root beer","blocks":[{"text":"Root beer is a North American beverage traditionally made using the root bark of the sassafras tree Sassafras albidum or the sarsaparilla vine Smilax ornata as the primary flavor. It started out as a type of small beer that was brewed. Today, root beer is usually a sweet, carbonated soft drink that is generally non-alcoholic and caffeine-free. It usually has a thick and foamy head."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Root_beer_in_glass_mug.jpg/330px-Root_beer_in_glass_mug.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_beer","wikidata":"Q36275"},{"title":"Root","blocks":[{"text":"In vascular plants, the roots are the organs of a plant that are modified to provide anchorage for the plant and take in water and nutrients into the plant body, which allows plants to grow taller and faster. They are most often below the surface of the soil, but roots can also be aerial or aerating, that is, growing up above the ground or especially above water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Primary_and_secondary_cotton_roots.jpg/330px-Primary_and_secondary_cotton_roots.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root","wikidata":"Q41500"},{"title":"Beer","blocks":[{"text":"Beer is an alcoholic beverage produced by the brewing and fermentation of starches from cereal grain—most commonly malted barley, although wheat, maize, rice, and oats are also used. The grain is mashed to convert starch in the grain to sugars, which dissolve in water to form wort. Fermentation of the wort by yeast produces ethanol and carbonation in the beer. Beer is one of the oldest and most widely consumed alcoholic drinks in the world, and one of the most popular of all drinks. Most modern beer is brewed with hops, which add bitterness and other flavours and act as a natural preservative and stabilizer. Other flavouring agents, such as gruit, herbs, or fruits, may be included or used instead of hops. In commercial brewing, natural carbonation is often replaced with forced carbonation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Jeju_beer_09_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Jeju_beer_09_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beer","wikidata":"Q44"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/All_gone_Could_I_have_another_glass_of_that_Hires%27_Rootbeer.jpg/500px-All_gone_Could_I_have_another_glass_of_that_Hires%27_Rootbeer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/All_gone_Could_I_have_another_glass_of_that_Hires%27_Rootbeer.jpg/500px-All_gone_Could_I_have_another_glass_of_that_Hires%27_Rootbeer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Hires' root beer advertisement from 1894","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAll_gone_Could_I_have_another_glass_of_that_Hires'_Rootbeer.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/CSIRO_ScienceImage_11626_Barley_root.jpg/500px-CSIRO_ScienceImage_11626_Barley_root.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/CSIRO_ScienceImage_11626_Barley_root.jpg/1280px-CSIRO_ScienceImage_11626_Barley_root.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/CSIRO_ScienceImage_11626_Barley_root.jpg/500px-CSIRO_ScienceImage_11626_Barley_root.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The cross-section of a barley root","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACSIRO_ScienceImage_11626_Barley_root.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ranunculus_Root_Cross_Section.png/500px-Ranunculus_Root_Cross_Section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Ranunculus_Root_Cross_Section.png/500px-Ranunculus_Root_Cross_Section.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ranunculus root cross section","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARanunculus_Root_Cross_Section.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Kiental_entre_Herrsching_y_Andechs%2C_Alemania_2012-05-01%2C_DD_12.JPG/500px-Kiental_entre_Herrsching_y_Andechs%2C_Alemania_2012-05-01%2C_DD_12.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Kiental_entre_Herrsching_y_Andechs%2C_Alemania_2012-05-01%2C_DD_12.JPG/1280px-Kiental_entre_Herrsching_y_Andechs%2C_Alemania_2012-05-01%2C_DD_12.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Kiental_entre_Herrsching_y_Andechs%2C_Alemania_2012-05-01%2C_DD_12.JPG/500px-Kiental_entre_Herrsching_y_Andechs%2C_Alemania_2012-05-01%2C_DD_12.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Large, mature tree roots above the soil","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%